{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01QdsZ4XQPYKY1f62G5J5Vxc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q5: Data parallelism for single-request assistant?**\n\n**Core answer:**\n- **Data parallelism is a training concept** — it splits a dataset across N GPU replicas (DDP/FSDP in PyTorch), each holding a full model copy, then AllReduces gradients. It scales training *throughput*, not inference *latency*.\n- For a **single-request personal assistant** (batch size = 1 at inference), data parallelism is irrelevant. The right levers are:\n  - **Streaming (SSE)** for sub-200ms TTFT\n  - **Quantization** (INT8/AWQ) to reduce memory bandwidth pressure\n  - **KV cache reuse** across conversation turns\n  - **Model tiering** (GPT-4o-mini / Llama 3 8B for routine turns)\n- The one inference-time parallelism that *does* help latency is **tensor parallelism** (split weight matrices across GPUs via Megatron/vLLM `--tensor-parallel-size`) — but only when the model doesn't fit on a single GPU.\n\n**Key pitfall caught:** Candidates often say \"data parallelism to speed up responses\" — the better answer clarifies training vs. inference contexts and names the actual latency levers."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":280,"cache_read_input_tokens":69047,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":280,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b60aad8f-3198-4e6e-b060-aa1f5788aa82","uuid":"9f9df51a-bc65-4880-a51b-d60a064521aa"}
